//! A typed gRPC client of the job service (`jobscheduler.v1.JobService`, generated by the
//! jobscheduler's `js-proto` crate), for the host executor.
//!
//! The job is the service's one public concept: the client submits one OCI image to run to
//! completion and reads its result. This crate wraps the six job calls and nothing else:
//! [`Client::submit_job`], [`Client::get_job`], [`Client::list_jobs`] (and
//! [`Client::list_all_jobs`], which follows the pages), [`Client::cancel_job`],
//! [`Client::get_result`] and [`Client::get_logs`]. It takes and returns `js-proto`'s own
//! messages ([`JobSpec`](js_proto::v1::JobSpec), [`Job`](js_proto::v1::Job),
//! [`JobResult`](js_proto::v1::JobResult), ...), so there is no second copy of them to keep in
//! step. The host, pool, provider and usage calls are not wrapped: no kbf code needs them, and
//! operators have the service's own CLI.
//!
//! A job id is checked before anything is sent: 1 to 63 bytes of letters, digits, dot,
//! underscore and dash. The caller chooses it and keeps it, because the id is what makes a
//! submit idempotent; the client never makes one up. The service is still the authority on
//! every rule.
//!
//! The host executor of `kbf-server` (`crates/kbf-server/src/executor`) is its one caller: it
//! submits each placed action as a job and reads its result, and the logs of a job that did
//! not end well whose result holds no output.
//!
//! # Safety of the credential
//!
//! The bearer token comes from a file only its owner can use ([`Token::from_file`]) and is
//! sent, in the `authorization` metadata of every call and marked sensitive so it is not
//! logged, to the one [`Origin`] the client was built for. `http` is accepted only for
//! loopback; this is the client refusing to send its token in clear, not a rule on the
//! server. For `https` the client trusts the one CA it is given and no system root, over TLS
//! 1.2 or 1.3 with the `ring` provider, and checks that the server's certificate carries the
//! origin's name; it presents no client certificate. A [`JobFile`](js_proto::v1::JobFile)'s
//! contents, which may be a presigned URL, never print: `js-proto` writes its `Debug`.
//!
//! # Errors
//!
//! [`Error`] separates what the caller does next. [`Error::Unreachable`] means nothing was
//! sent. [`Error::Transport`] and [`Error::Decode`] mean the server may have acted
//! ([`Error::may_have_applied`]); since `SubmitJob` is idempotent on the job id and
//! `CancelJob` on the job, the caller repeats the same call. [`Error::Refused`] is a refused
//! submit with its [`RefusalCode`](js_proto::v1::RefusalCode); [`Error::Status`] is any other
//! answer that says the server did not act (`NOT_FOUND` for a job unknown or past its
//! retention window, `UNAUTHENTICATED`, `UNIMPLEMENTED` from an older server, ...). The module
//! `error` holds the table that classes each of the 17 gRPC codes. The client never retries
//! on its own.
//!
//! # The fake
//!
//! With the `fake` feature, [`fake::FakeJobServer`] is a job service on loopback that keeps
//! the service's rules: an idempotent submit and its `job-id-conflict`, admission against the
//! hosts and offers the test gives it (`no-capable-host`, the two wall-limit refusals), the
//! states of a job moved by the test one step at a time, cancel as the design has it, results
//! and logs kept for the retention window on the fake's own clock, host silence on `GetJob`,
//! file contents never returned, the bearer token required on every call, and faults of the
//! connection. It exists so that the code above this crate is tested against the service's
//! contract and not against a mock that agrees with it. `tests/golden/` holds the service's
//! bytes, copied from `js-proto` where it has them.
#![forbid(unsafe_code)]

